<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif;">
    <h2 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h2>
    <p style="background-color: #f0f0f0; color: #333; padding: 5px; text-align: center; margin-top: 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 70%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Visiting the Panama Canal</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> A Vessel Is Crossing!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Perform multi-step tasks based on oral directions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can extract specific information from talks about the Panama Canal (e.g., 'What does the Panama Canal connect?' 'The Panama Canal connects the Atlantic Ocean to the Pacific Ocean.').<br>Can follow simple, oral, multi-step directions related to an oral description of a visit to the Panama Canal (e.g., 'Draw the canal and color it blue, and then label the two oceans.' 'Color the largest vessel red, and then draw small waves.').<br>Can get the main points of a simple talk about the Panama Canal ('Private boats can go through the canal.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows students large, colorful pictures of the Panama Canal, different types of vessels (ship, boat, yacht), oceans (Atlantic, Pacific), and locks. The teacher points to each picture and asks, "What do you see?" and guides students to respond with simple words like "ship," "canal," "ocean." The teacher asks, "Is it big or small?" to introduce comparative adjectives in a simple way.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words: 'canal' (/kəˈnæl/), 'boat' (/boʊt/), 'ocean' (/ˈoʊʃən/), 'vessel' (/ˈvɛsəl/), 'ship' (/ʃɪp/). The teacher pronounces the words slowly and clearly, emphasizing the /k/ sound in 'Canal' and the /b/ sound in 'boat'. The teacher uses actions (e.g., drawing a wave in the air for 'wave') and points to flashcards to ensure visual association. The teacher models simple sentences like "The canal is big." "A vessel is crossing."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple concept checking questions and examples.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does a boat fly or travel on water?" (Expected answer: "Travel on water.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the Panama Canal a river or a canal?" (Expected answer: "A canal.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher will also model blending sounds for 'boat' by saying /b/ /oʊt/ and asking students to blend the sounds to form the word.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Visiting the Panama Canal."</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio description or teacher-read narrative excerpt about visiting the Panama Canal. The audio will describe current events and facts in simple language. For example: "Hello, children! We are visiting the Panama Canal today. Look! A big vessel is crossing the Canal now. It travels from the Atlantic Ocean to the Pacific Ocean. The Panama Canal connects two big oceans. Many people are here to observe the ships. That black vessel is bigger than the white ship. It is important and historic." (Genre: Short descriptive narrative. Register: Informal, informative. Format: Audio recording or teacher-read script).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing current events (e.g., 'A big vessel is crossing the Canal now.') and Describing facts (e.g., 'The Panama Canal connects two oceans.'). Comparing things (e.g., 'That black vessel is bigger than the white ship.').</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio twice.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question (First Listening):</b> The teacher asks, "What place are we visiting?" (Students can point to a picture of the Panama Canal or say "Panama Canal").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Second Listening):</b> The teacher presents simple True/False statements orally, and students show thumbs up for True, thumbs down for False.</li>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"A vessel is flying across the Canal." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The Canal connects two oceans." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The Panama Canal is small." (False)</li>
                        </ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Second Listening):</b> The teacher says, "Listen: What does the Panama Canal connect?" Students listen for the answer and the teacher can provide image options or model the answer "The Atlantic Ocean and the Pacific Ocean."</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice listening for specific vocabulary (canal, boat, ocean, vessel, ship, travel, connect, big, small) and grammatical structures (Present simple for facts: 'The Panama Canal connects two oceans.'; Present continuous for current events: 'A vessel is crossing now.'; Comparatives: 'bigger than', 'smaller than'). Phonemic awareness practice: spelling 'canal,' 'vessel,' 'wave.' Pragmatic function: Describing facts and comparing things.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Listen and Point:</b> The teacher gives oral instructions, and students point to the correct picture on their worksheet (e.g., pictures of different vessels, the canal, oceans). For example: "Point to the big ship." "Point to the Panama Canal." "Point to the ocean." "Point to the yacht." The teacher also asks, "Which word is longer: 'boat' or 'vessel'?" (Students listen and identify).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled - Listen and Choose:</b> The teacher says a sentence with a missing word (aural cloze), and students listen and choose the correct vocabulary word from two spoken options. Example: "The Panama _______ connects two oceans. (canal / river)" (Students say "canal"). "A small _______ is moving. (boat / car)" (Students say "boat"). The teacher models spelling "C-A-N-A-L" and asks students to spell simple words related to the scenario.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Listen and Verify:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher gives a multi-step oral instruction for each pair, e.g., "Student A, listen: 'Draw a big ship. Color it red. It is crossing the canal.' Student B, listen carefully, then say 'Yes, you drew a big, red ship crossing the canal' or 'No, that is not a red ship.' " Students practice attentive listening and then confirm/correct. This incorporates respectful conversations by listening and responding to a partner's statement.</li>
                    </ol>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Visiting the Panama Canal."</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Students will complete a drawing and labeling activity based on multi-step oral directions given by the teacher. This task directly assesses the learning outcome of following simple, multi-step oral directions related to a visit to the Panama Canal.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> The teacher distributes a blank paper or a simple outline of a canal. The teacher gives the following oral directions, pausing between each step to allow students time to process and perform. The teacher repeats each direction clearly twice.</p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"First, draw the Panama Canal across the paper. Make it wide."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Next, color the water in the canal blue."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Then, draw a very big vessel in the canal. This vessel is bigger than a yacht."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Now, draw a small boat next to the big vessel."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"After that, label one side 'Atlantic Ocean' and the other side 'Pacific Ocean'." (Teacher writes these words on the board for visual aid after giving the oral instruction.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Finally, draw small waves in the water around the vessels."</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Throughout the activity, the teacher describes current events, "The students are drawing the canal. They are coloring the water blue."</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: center;">8-10 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher collects the students' completed drawings from the Production stage. The teacher will review each drawing to see how accurately students followed the oral, multi-step directions.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate following of 3+ oral directions (e.g., canal drawn, water colored blue, vessel/boat drawn, oceans labeled).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inclusion of target vocabulary elements (canal, vessel, boat, ocean).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrated understanding of comparative concepts (big vessel, small boat).</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a simple exit ticket by circling or coloring a happy/neutral/sad face for each statement, or by marking 'Yes/No/A little'.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can understand words about the Panama Canal."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can follow the teacher's directions to draw a picture."</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> The teacher asks the students:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you hear today about the Panama Canal?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to follow the drawing directions?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening lesson? (e.g., visual aids, clear modeling, repetitive oral instructions).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to improve listening comprehension for the students? (e.g., more pre-teaching of vocabulary, slower pace, simpler sentence structures, more opportunities for TPR).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students engage in respectful conversations during pair activities?</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension), students will read a short text about the Panama Canal, building upon the vocabulary and concepts introduced in this listening lesson. The same vocabulary words (canal, vessel, ocean, connect, travel, big, small) will be present in the reading material, allowing for cohesive progression.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: center;">2-3 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>